<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05A7">Example <span data-tag="05A7">4.8.5.19</span>.</a> Let $n \geq -2$ be an integer and let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a functor of $\infty $-categories which exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ as a local $n$-truncation of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (see Definition <a data-tag="058C" href="/tag/058C">4.7.6.13</a>). Then $F$ is categorically $(n+2)$-connective. To prove this, we may assume without loss of generality that $F$ exhibits $\operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ as the $(n+2)$-coskeleton of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (Proposition <a data-tag="058G" href="/tag/058G">4.7.6.19</a>). In this case, $F$ is bijective on $m$-simplices for $m \leq n+2$, so the desired result follows from Proposition <a data-tag="05CB" href="/tag/05CB">4.8.5.18</a>. </p>
</article>
